UPDATE public.designers SET biography = $bio$The source of inspiration for Celso de Lemos is the artisanal craft of Portugal, its rich history interwoven into every fabric, with master weavers guiding their skills across generations. The maison was founded by Celso de Lemos, a chemical engineer trained in Belgium who began his career producing bath rugs by hand from his parents' garage in Viseu and went on to build the luxury linen house Abyss & Habidecor.

Based in north-central Portugal, the maison produces exceptional bed linens, bath textiles and home accessories from <em>Giza</em> extra-long-staple cotton — a rare fibre that represents a tiny fraction of global cotton production. Every collection is made to order in Portugal, with an in-house chemist monitoring dye-bath pH to guarantee colour consistency across an unusually broad palette. Named lines such as <em>Secret Bedding</em> illustrate the house's quiet, tactile approach to luxury.

Celso de Lemos himself remains famously discreet, preferring to keep the spotlight on his craftspeople. A man of multiple métiers, he also stewards <em>Quinta de Lemos</em>, a vineyard estate in Silgueiros with a guest house and a Michelin-starred restaurant — extending the same exacting standards of land, terroir and craft to every dimension of his work.$bio$
WHERE id = 'ed087fe1-1f80-4561-96aa-1b713df62f67';

UPDATE public.designers SET biography = $bio$Born in Beirut in 1982, Charles Kalpakian trained in product design in France before apprenticing at the Ora-Ïto agency in Paris, one of the most conceptually driven studios of its generation. After a period assisting the architects Leberre and Guillois and collaborating with Frédéric Ruyan, he founded his own studio in Bagnolet in 2011.

His vocabulary fuses chiselled geometry with the decorative memory of his Lebanese roots — calligraphy, marquetry, the architecture of the Levant — filtered through a resolutely contemporary, urban culture. The <em>Cinétisme</em> wall pieces, with their hammered, brushed, guilloché and lacquered aluminium panels, are emblematic: optical illusions that turn flat surfaces into hypnotic, almost kinetic reliefs.

Kalpakian designs for <a href="/designers/haymann-editions">Haymann Editions</a> — including the <em>Max</em> and <em>Frank</em> seating series — as well as for La Chance Paris and Brussels gallery Galerie BSL, where his retro-futurist <em>Moon</em> armchairs and <em>Crescent</em> sofas reinterpret mid-century classics with a sci-fi geometry entirely his own.$bio$
WHERE id = '3a2b1926-6310-453f-bc6d-116e53975895';

UPDATE public.designers SET biography = $bio$Félix Agostini (1910–1980) was a Parisian sculptor and lighting designer whose figural bronze lamps have become some of the most sought-after objects of the French post-war period. Trained as a draughtsman and illustrator at the renowned printing house Draeger — where he produced advertising work for Peugeot, Ford and Chanel — he turned in the mid-1950s to bronze sculpture, opening a workshop on rue de Penthièvre in the 8th arrondissement of Paris.

From 1955 onwards he modelled lamps and small sculptures in plaster, then cast them in bronze, building a personal vocabulary of caryatids, mythological figures and stylised animals. He collaborated with Diego Giacometti and paid tribute to both Giacometti brothers in his <em>Échassier</em> floor lamp. Every summer for several years he exhibited at the Galerie Cusin in Mougins, where much of his collectors' circle was formed. He produced close to 200 works in his lifetime, almost always outside prevailing artistic trends.

Since 2013, <a href="/designers/charles-paris">Charles Paris</a> — the historic Parisian bronze d'art house founded in 1908 — has held exclusive edition rights to his work, casting numbered and signed pieces from the original models in strict accordance with traditional bronze techniques. The maison's 2026 dual exhibition honouring Chrystiane Charles and Félix Agostini reaffirmed his place in the lineage of French sculptors who, like the Giacomettis, made of the lamp an object of pure sculpture.$bio$
WHERE id = 'bb08e592-73ca-4abc-9c25-cd60dd7458dc';

UPDATE public.designers SET biography = $bio$Born in Paris in 1966, Christophe Delcourt is a self-taught designer and éditeur whose path to furniture began in the theatre. Trained as an actor at the Cours Florent, he worked first in scenography — composing stage sets as arrangements of material, light and emotion — before learning his trade hands-on alongside a cabinetmaker and a blacksmith.

He founded Delcourt Collection in 1995, presenting his first prototypes at Maison & Objet that same year. Self-production was an identity rather than a strategy: each piece was made by hand, to order, in France. Neiman Marcus picked up his work within months; in 1998 he opened his first gallery in the Marais, later relocating his Paris flagship to the Left Bank on rue de Babylone.

Three decades on, the Delcourt Collection catalogue spans more than five hundred designs — tables, seating, lighting, cabinetry, textiles — including signature pieces such as the <em>EKO</em> sofa and the <em>IBO</em> coffee table, and recent thematic collections like <em>At the Edge of the Woods</em> and <em>Horses in My Dreams</em>. The house now also publishes guest designers, and in 2025 marked its thirtieth anniversary with a 250 m² flagship in Mumbai, becoming the first French luxury furniture house to establish a standalone presence in India.$bio$
WHERE id = 'a35d66a5-ec10-459b-9e88-bf31292d196e';

UPDATE public.designers SET biography = $bio$Cluzel Pluchon is the Lille-based studio of Sébastien Cluzel and Morgane Pluchon, both graduates of the École Supérieure d'Art et Design de Saint-Étienne (ESADSE) and ECAL in Lausanne. They launched their practice in 2015 at Salone Satellite in Milan, the platform that traditionally introduces emerging designers within the Salone del Mobile.

Their work is grounded in two convictions: that design should be accessible to everyone, and that it must remain at the service of the human. Through patient interrogation of material, form, colour and pattern, they design objects intended to <em>cue memories, invoke moments in history and redefine the expectations of the everyday</em>, in their own words.

For <a href="/designers/theoreme-editions">Théorème Editions</a> they conceived the <em>XT100</em> lighting system (2022), part of a wider portfolio that includes <em>Rune</em> for Corbery Editions, <em>Optica</em> glassware for Kimoto Glass Tokyo, <em>Plana</em> for Authentic Materials and seating for Giormani. The studio has been recognised with the Interieur Award at the Kortrijk Biennale (2018), the Maison & Objet Rising Talents Award (2023), and three FD100 Awards (2019, 2022, 2024).$bio$
WHERE id = 'fa4291a1-241e-49a0-8df6-655acbab48d7';

UPDATE public.designers SET biography = $bio$Cocorico Paris is the Parisian studio of Mathieu Girard and Gauthier Pouillart, founded in 2011 and awarded the Prix Cinna in 2013. Both trained as graphic designers and interior architects, and the studio works across furniture, spatial concepts and brand identity.

Their seating draws openly on the masters of the twentieth century — the round, enveloping silhouettes of Charles and Ray Eames, the tubular, industrial logic of Jean Prouvé — without nostalgia. For <a href="/designers/haymann-editions">Haymann Editions</a>, the duo paired these references with Gabriel fabrics in pop-inflected tones of pinkish purple, brick red and mustard yellow, using seam lines as relief to play with shadow and light.

Beyond their hospitality work for Haymann, Cocorico has designed pieces for La Chance Paris, Galerie Philia and Cider Edition — among them the <em>Onigiri</em> sofa, the <em>Montmartre</em> stair seat and the <em>Les Baigneurs</em> decorative object — building a vocabulary that is at once historically literate and resolutely contemporary.$bio$
WHERE id = 'e9b44fda-2222-4669-a65d-8d1160d9284d';

UPDATE public.designers SET biography = $bio$Born in 1980 in Recreo, a small town in the Catamarca province of north-western Argentina, Cristián Mohaded grew up in a family of makers — his mother weaving, his grandmother painting watercolours, local artisans working at the street fair. He studied Industrial Design at the National University of Córdoba, citing Ricardo Blanco, the Campana Brothers and Lucio Fontana among his early references.

In 2008 he founded Mohaded Studio in Buenos Aires as <em>a hybrid practice where crafts, industry, tradition and innovation coexist</em>. Today he works between Buenos Aires and Milan, designing objects, furniture, lighting and site-specific installations that draw directly on the landscapes of his childhood — the mountains, salt flats, sand dunes and volcanoes of the Argentine north-west — and on long-standing collaborations with indigenous Argentine artisans.

His <em>Para</em> chair for <a href="/designers/la-chance-paris">La Chance</a> is among his best-known editions; further afield, he has designed for Louis Vuitton's <em>Objets Nomades</em> collection and presented the acclaimed <em>Territorio Híbrido</em> exhibition. In 2026 he was named <em>EDIDA Designer of the Year</em> at Milan Design Week, the Elle Decoration International Design Awards jury citing his singular reading of the codes of his Argentine homeland.$bio$
WHERE id = '3eb37590-2ed6-4ee0-8601-00cc5f876862';

UPDATE public.designers SET biography = $bio$Cristoforo Trapani is not a designer by training but a Michelin-starred Italian chef, at the helm of <em>La Magnolia</em> in Forte dei Marmi on the Tuscan coast. His culinary identity was shaped by early contact with simple local ingredients and refined through formative encounters with chefs Heinz Beck, Antonino Cannavacciuolo, Moreno Cedroni and Davide Scabin.

For <a href="/designers/mmairo">MMairo</a>, the Italian marble house that invites <em>heterogeneous authors</em> — architects, designers and, in his case, a chef — to translate their personal imaginaries into domestic objects, Trapani conceived two pieces that bring a cook's eye to the dining table.

The <em>Memory</em> bowl is described as an ancestral reminder of the vital relationship between people and food, equally suited to soups, fruit salads or dried fruit; the <em>Magnolia</em> table is conceived around the idea that a chef constructs culinary compositions and therefore designs the very surfaces on which they are presented. Both are sculpted from marble — including Black Marquinia — extending the precision and restraint of his cuisine into form.$bio$
WHERE id = '900bc8be-7fb8-4392-93e8-2c0a351e9ed0';

UPDATE public.designers SET biography = $bio$Born in Jerusalem in 1971, Dan Yeffet studied Industrial Design at the Bezalel Academy of Art and Design before moving to the Netherlands to complete his training at the Gerrit Rietveld Academie in Amsterdam. He established his first studio in Amsterdam and, in 2005, relocated his practice to Paris, where he continues to work today.

His work moves freely between space and object — furniture, lighting, glass sculpture, textiles, conceptual installations and urban projects — with a recurring interest in honest materials and clear, sculptural silhouettes. Between 2010 and 2012 he collaborated with Czech designer Lucie Koldová on a number of significant projects.

For <a href="/designers/haymann-editions">Haymann Editions</a> he conceived the <em>Tribute</em> lamp — an homage to Louis Poulsen's PH5 — in combinations of white Carrara or Black Marquinia marble with brass, powder-coated steel and smoked glass, alongside the <em>Wave</em> seating series. His work is held in collections including the V&A in London, the Design Museum Holon, the Shanghai Museum of Glass and the FNAC Foundation in Paris, and has been recognised with the FNAC First Prize (2014), two ELLE Décor International Design Awards (2010, 2012) and the Czech Grand Design Award for Designer of the Year (2012).$bio$
WHERE id = 'bd83fd45-d5d4-4789-a9c6-e8538e374741';

UPDATE public.designers SET biography = $bio$David Raffoul and Nicolas Moussallem met as architecture students at the Lebanese Academy of Fine Arts (ALBA) in Beirut in 2006, and pursued a master's in product design together at the Scuola Politecnica di Design in Milan before founding david/nicolas in Beirut in 2011. The studio has since opened a second office in Milan (2021) and a third in San Francisco (2023).

Their language is openly <em>retrofuturist</em> — a controlled collision of design history periods that produces objects at once new and strangely familiar. The duo work from opposite temperaments: Moussallem is the detail obsessive, Raffoul the freethinker. One starts from a narrative; the other sketches chunks of form. Carved wood and marquetry recur throughout their output as quiet but unmistakable signatures.

For <a href="/designers/haymann-editions">Haymann Editions</a> they have designed pieces including the <em>Lio</em> collection of consoles and side tables and the <em>Fondale</em> lacquered desk. They are also represented by Carpenters Workshop Gallery in London, Paris and New York, by Nilufar in Milan, and by Iwan Maktabi in Beirut, with editorial features in <em>Architectural Digest</em> and a regular presence at Maison & Objet.$bio$
WHERE id = '7a506526-ae44-4c76-8694-210409c81aa6';